# AI Coding Assistant Rules for Silver Wolf VI

## 1. Feature Preservation & Regression Prevention
When implementing new architectural patterns (like replacing old UIs with new React components or integrating plugins), you MUST NOT blindly delete, overwrite, or "lose" micro-interactions and aesthetic features.
- **Do not lose custom UI features** like custom cursor movements, animations, or styling.
- Always audit the existing code you are replacing for unique behaviors (e.g., custom `mousemove` trackers, glowing effects, specific easing functions) and port them over to the new implementation.

## 2. Aesthetics & Theming
- The app uses a highly customized "Honkai Star Rail" space opera theme. The primary aesthetic color is Silver Wolf Purple/Magenta (`#8A5BC7`).
- Do not default to generic UI frameworks or plain colors. Maintain the glassmorphism panels, blurry backgrounds, and high-tech typography (`JetBrains Mono`, `Outfit`).
- Always check `src/lib/themeEngine.ts` and `src/index.css` before hardcoding colors. Custom styling must read from CSS variables (e.g., `var(--theme-primary)`) to support dynamic live-theming.

## 3. React 19 & TypeScript Standards
- **React 19 Compatibility:** Follow React 19 patterns.
  - Refs can be passed directly as props (no need for `forwardRef`).
  - Use modern hook/state hooks where appropriate, and ensure TypeScript definitions match React 19 types (e.g. `React.JSX.Element` instead of `JSX.Element`).
  - Use clean hooks and separate UI markup from side-effects and business logic.
- **TypeScript Strictness:** Maintain strict typing. Avoid using `any`. Write proper interface definitions for all component props, stores, and hooks.

## 4. Tailwind CSS v4 & Styling Integration
- The project integrates Tailwind CSS v4 via `@import "tailwindcss"` in `src/index.css` alongside `@theme` variables.
- Direct tailwind utilities can be used, but always reference the themed variables (e.g. `bg-primary`, `text-text-main`, `border-panel-border`) defined in `@theme` to ensure theme changes are respected.
- Do not bypass the dynamic CSS theme variables mapped to the document element.

## 5. Zustand State & Local Chat Storage Integrity
- **Zustand Store (`src/store/uiStore.ts`):** Global settings (e.g., active palette, UI opacity, motion enabled, sound effects, high load throttling status) must be read from and written to the Zustand store.
- **Chat Persistence:** Local history is managed by `src/hooks/useChatPersistence.ts` and stored under `silverWolf.chatHistory`. Any changes to the message schema MUST include fallback or migration logic to prevent crashes for existing users.

## 6. Simulated Telemetry & High-Load Throttling System
- **Load Scaling:** The app features a performance self-regulation system (e.g., turning off particles, canvas effects, and heavy transitions when CPU/load exceeds threshold).
- **High-Load Flag:** Always respect `isHighLoad` state and check for `prefers-reduced-motion` media queries/toggles.
- Do not remove or break the telemetry data hooks or component throttling logic.

## 7. Cesium & 3D Integration
- The 3D globe relies on `CesiumJS`. You must strictly maintain the single-mounted persistent `viewer` architecture to prevent WebGL context loss and slow loading.
- High-resolution textures (e.g., ArcGIS World Imagery) must be preserved in the fallback chain in `src/lib/imageryFactory.ts`.

## 8. Custom Cursor Movement
- The app utilizes a custom cursor movement approach (fluid trailing dot/glow effects).
- Ensure that any new overlay components or interactive elements correctly interact with the global custom cursor. Do not apply `cursor-default` randomly if it overrides the custom implementation.

## 9. No Placeholder Images
- Never reference generic placeholders like `/placeholder.png` or `https://via.placeholder.com`. If an image asset is required, request image generation or use a high-quality, relevant illustration.
